## Proposal: Cinemarr

  Working title: Cinemarr, mod ID cinemarr. It fits alongside
  Jammarr without placing the Plex trademark in the mod’s name.

  Cinemarr would provide server-authoritative Plex movie and TV
  playback on player-built screens. The dedicated server owns Plex
  access, library permissions, playback sessions, and
  synchronization; clients decode the stream and render it in-world.

  Plex Media Server
          ↓ one controlled transcode per active TV/session
  Minecraft server
          ↓ authenticated, bounded media segments
  Tracking clients
          ↓ decode once into a dynamic texture
  Player-built pixel screen + positional audio

  It should be a separate sibling repository, but reuse Jammarr’s
  proven architectural ideas from PAmpMod/README.md: Java-8-
  compatible shared policy code, platform adapters, server-owned
  credentials, bounded chunk transport, hashing, retry gates, clock
  synchronization, rate limiting, sanitized errors, and strong
  release gates. It should not require Jammarr at runtime.

  ## Building a TV

  The basic blocks would be:

  - Screen Pixel: one full block equals exactly one logical video
    pixel.

  - TV Controller: placed behind or beside the screen; holds
    ownership, tuning, and playback state.

  - Optional casing, speaker, remote, and redstone receiver blocks
    that do not count toward resolution.

  A 4×4 arrangement is therefore genuinely a 4×4 display. A 2000×20
  arrangement is a 2000×20 display.

  Screen rules:

  - All pixels must lie on one plane and face the same direction.
  - Any four-directionally connected shape is valid.
  - Rectangles, L-shapes, circles, holes, notches, and other
    connected silhouettes work.

  - The shape’s bounding box defines its logical resolution; missing
    blocks form a visibility mask.

  - Disconnected islands are separate TVs unless joined with pixels.
  - Screens can face north, south, east, west, upward, or downward.

  Pixel membership should be recorded as blocks are placed or
  removed in world saved data. Activation must not require all
  chunks of a 2000-block-wide TV to be loaded simultaneously. Pixel
  blocks should remain ordinary blocks, not 40,000 ticking block
  entities.

  On the client, each visible screen section is batched into a mesh
  sampling one shared dynamic texture. No block states, light
  updates, or world packets change every frame.

  Suggested default construction limits:

   Limit                            Default
  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  ━━━━━━━━━
   Minimum pixels                         4
  ───────────────────────────────  ─────────
   Maximum pixels per TV             65,536
  ───────────────────────────────  ─────────
   Maximum width or height            2,048
  ───────────────────────────────  ─────────
   Maximum concurrent active TVs          2
  ───────────────────────────────  ─────────
   Maximum screens per owner              4

  That admits both 4×4 and 2000×20 while excluding multi-million-
  block screens by default. Operators can change the limits.
  Cinemarr would never force-load the whole structure; only
  currently tracked portions render.

  Make sure there are blocks that can be "quick" shortcuts to common resolution TVs
	- examples: 144, 240, 480, 720, 1080, 1440, 4k, 8k, TV Blocks that automatically make a TV multiblock of matching resolution.
	- These should require appropriate amount of resources and have config options to disable

  ## Exact and unusual resolutions

  The TV has three presentation modes:

  - Fit: preserve aspect ratio and letterbox.
  - Fill: preserve aspect ratio and crop.
  - Stretch: independently scale width and height to occupy every
    constructed pixel.

  Stretch makes a 2000×20 television intentionally produce an
  extremely wide, distorted image. Fit and fill give less distorted
  alternatives.

  Plex can prepare compatible H.264/AAC streams and downscale source
  media, but its documented automatic-quality behavior does not
  upscale video. Exact arbitrary dimensions also should not be
  treated as a stable Plex transcoder contract. Plex describes
  transcoding as adapting resolution and codecs
  (https://support.plex.tv/articles/200430303-streaming-overview/)
  and explicitly says converted video is not upscaled beyond its
  source resolution in automatic quality handling. Plex’s
  transcoding behavior also depends on server capability and
  configured limits (https://support.plex.tv/articles/transcoder/).

  Therefore:

  1. Cinemarr requests the nearest sensible Plex rendition using an
     even, codec-safe resolution and bitrate.

  2. The client decodes that rendition.
  3. The client performs the final resampling into the exact TV
     raster.

  4. Odd encoded dimensions are padded to codec-safe dimensions and
     cropped before display.

  5. The screen mask removes pixels where the player did not place
     blocks.

  Examples:

  - 4×4: Plex might supply a small conventional rendition such as
    320×180; Cinemarr downsamples it to precisely 4×4.

  - 2000×20: Plex supplies a practical rendition near the source
    resolution; Cinemarr performs the final 2000×20 stretch, fit, or
    crop.

  - 17×11 L-shaped TV: Cinemarr produces a 17×11 frame and masks out
    the absent blocks.

  This supports arbitrary logical output without depending on
  undocumented Plex behavior.

  ## Plex libraries and controls

  The server generates:

  - world/serverconfig/cinemarr-server.toml
  - world/serverconfig/cinemarr-libraries.toml

  Credentials remain server-side, preferably through
  CINEMARR_PLEX_TOKEN.

  An allowlist entry could contain:

  [[libraries]]
  id = "family_movies"
  section = "Movies"
  displayName = "Family Movies"
  allowMovies = true
  allowShows = false
  maximumContentRating = "PG-13"
  permissionLevel = 0

  [[libraries]]
  id = "television"
  section = "TV Shows"
  displayName = "Television"
  allowMovies = false
  allowShows = true
  permissionLevel = 0

  Players only see configured libraries. The UI supports:

  - Movie browsing and search.
  - Shows → seasons → episodes.
  - Continue from the current episode.
  - Queue and now-playing views.
  - Audio-track and subtitle selection.
  - Fit/fill/stretch controls.
  - Local volume and local screen-disable options.
  - Operators or TV owners controlling play, pause, seek, skip,
    stop, and queue mutation, according to config.

  Multiple TVs may tune into the same named watch-party session.
  Those TVs share one Plex transcode and timeline. Independent TVs
  consume separate transcode slots.

  ## Streaming architecture

  The Minecraft server opens one Plex session per distinct active
  playback session and keeps a short rolling cache of compressed
  segments. Clients never receive the Plex URL or token and do not
  need network access to Plex.

  Transport should inherit Jammarr’s mechanics:

  - Segments divided into Minecraft-safe payloads, likely 16 KiB.
  - Per-segment SHA-256 verification.
  - Pull-based acknowledged windows.
  - Strict buffering, request-rate, and playback-lead limits.
  - Keyframe index for late joins and seeking.
  - Presentation timestamps and filtered server-clock estimation.
  - Generation/session IDs so stale asynchronous work cannot start
    an old video.

  - Transcode shutdown when stopped or when no viewers remain for a
    configurable period.

  Audio is decoded from the same stream, synchronized to video, and
  emitted positionally from the TV. For very large TVs, the sound
  origin should be the nearest point on the screen rather than its
  controller.

  Only players tracking at least one screen chunk receive that TV’s
  media. A single compressed stream is fetched from Plex, but
  Minecraft-server upload still scales with the number of watching
  clients.

  ## Principal technical risk

  Video decoding is substantially harder than Jammarr’s pure-Java
  MP3 path. Minecraft does not expose a general video decoder.

  The recommended implementation is an LGPL-compatible FFmpeg/
  libavcodec client runtime with narrowly packaged native libraries
  for supported operating systems and architectures. The server
  merely relays compressed H.264/AAC segments and does not need
  native decoding.

  Before full implementation, a technical spike should prove:

  - Plex H.264/AAC session creation and clean termination.
  - Seekable/keyframed segment relay.
  - Native decoder packaging on Linux and Windows.
  - OpenGL texture upload without frame-time spikes.
  - Audio/video synchronization across two clients.
  - Licensing and final JAR size.

  A pure-Java decoder could be evaluated, but I would not select it
  without real 1080p performance and codec-compatibility evidence.
  Forge 1.7.10 should consequently be the final port, not the
  initial target.

  ## Delivery plan

  1. Feasibility spike: live Plex transcode, decoder, texture
     upload, synchronized audio, 4×4 and 2000×20 synthetic screens.

  2. NeoForge 1.21.1 MVP: movies/episodes, one TV session, planar
     arbitrary shapes, fit/fill/stretch, server library allowlist,
     positional audio.

  3. Production hardening: multi-TV limits, watch parties, seeking,
     subtitles, persistence, reconnects, rolling cache, diagnostics,
     permissions.

  4. Modern loader matrix: align with Jammarr’s Fabric/Quilt, Forge,
     and NeoForge families.

  5. Forge 1.7.10: isolated Java 8 adapter and decoder/renderer
     validation.

  6. Release gates: fake-Plex tests, geometry property tests,
     GameTests, deterministic frame hashes, two-client A/V
     synchronization, giant-screen chunk-boundary tests, malformed-
     stream handling, and live Plex validation.

  The most important acceptance test is visual: two real clients
  must show the same identifiable frame with synchronized audible
  output. Successful transcode startup or texture allocation alone
  would not count.
